infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title IPv4 vs IPv6 header: what got dropped
  desc IPv6 trades a bigger fixed header for less per-hop router work
  compares
    - label IPv4 header
      children
        - label 20-byte minimum header
          desc Variable length; grows when options are present
        - label Header checksum, recomputed every hop
          desc Covers the header only, but TTL changes so each router redoes it
        - label Routers may fragment in transit
          desc Splits oversized packets, adding CPU work and security holes
        - label Options inline in the header
          desc Bloats the fixed part; IHL field tracks the length
        - label TTL caps hops, Protocol picks L4
          desc 8-bit hop counter; Protocol=6 TCP, 17 UDP, 1 ICMP
    - label IPv6 header
      children
        - label 40-byte fixed header
          desc Always the same size; addresses alone are 16 bytes each
        - label No header checksum at all
          desc Relies on L2 (Ethernet FCS) + L4 (TCP/UDP) for integrity
        - label Routers never fragment
          desc Too-big packet returns ICMP; sender retransmits smaller
        - label Options chain via Next Header
          desc Extension headers (Routing, Fragment) appended, not inline
        - label Hop Limit = TTL, Next Header = Protocol
          desc Same roles renamed; plus a 20-bit Flow Label for ECMP
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6
